virgl: no alpha-only render targets on hosts that keep GL_ALPHA natively.

The firmware's Qt Quick 5.0 distance-field glyph cache grows its GL_ALPHA atlas by drawing the old
one into an RGBA texture and glCopyTexSubImage2D-ing that into the new GL_ALPHA texture
(tests/virgl/glyph-cache.c). st turns the copy into pipe->blit(RGBA8 -> A8) whenever A8 is a
render target, and virgl sends that blit to the host as it is.

virglrenderer 1.1.0 keeps A8 as GL_ALPHA8 when the host context accepts it (la_formats_compat,
vrend_formats.c:197, tried before the GL_R8 + swizzle fallback at :167), which a desktop context
with the compatibility profile does: QEMU asks for a core profile, but V3D's highest desktop
version is 3.1, where the profile is ignored and Mesa hands out 3.1 with GL_ARB_compatibility.
GL_ALPHA8 is framebuffer-complete there, so A8 is advertised as a render target, and with no
swizzle on either format (vrend_blit_needs_swizzle, vrend_renderer.c:1055) the blit takes
vrend_renderer_blit_fbo, a glBlitFramebuffer from the RGBA8 texture into the GL_ALPHA8 one. On V3D
(Mesa 26.2) that converting glBlitFramebuffer writes alpha 0; drawing into the same texture, a
glCopyTexSubImage2D into it and an ALPHA8 -> ALPHA8 blit are right. The text of the no-signal
screensaver disappears when its atlas grows. A core-profile host (d3d12, llvmpipe) rejects
GL_ALPHA8, stores A8 as GL_R8 with a 000R swizzle, and blits with a shader that writes the
source's alpha into red: correct.

So on a host whose alpha formats are native, alpha-only formats are no render targets, and st
copies into them on the CPU (fallback_copy_texsubimage; mipmaps and conversions likewise). The
host's caps carry no "A8 is emulated" bit; A16_FLOAT and A32_FLOAT are in vrend only as
GL_ALPHA16F/32F of la_formats_compat with no fallback, so a sampleable one of them means the
compatibility formats were accepted. Core-profile and GLES hosts keep the blit.
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/virgl/virgl_screen.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/virgl/virgl_screen.c
@@ -700,6 +700,22 @@
                                       caps->v1.render.bitmask,
                                       may_emulate_bgra))
          return false;
+
+      /* Alpha-only render targets on a host that keeps GL_ALPHA* natively
+       * (a desktop context with the compatibility profile, e.g. V3D's GL 3.1):
+       * vrend then turns a blit into them into glBlitFramebuffer, and V3D's
+       * converting glBlitFramebuffer into GL_ALPHA8 writes 0. Without the
+       * render target st copies into alpha textures on the CPU. A host that
+       * emulates alpha as R8 with a swizzle (a core context) blits with a
+       * shader and keeps it. GL_ALPHA16F/32F have no such emulation in vrend,
+       * so a sampleable A16_FLOAT or A32_FLOAT says the host is the former.
+       */
+      if (util_format_is_alpha(format) &&
+          (virgl_format_check_bitmask(PIPE_FORMAT_A16_FLOAT,
+                                      caps->v1.sampler.bitmask, false) ||
+           virgl_format_check_bitmask(PIPE_FORMAT_A32_FLOAT,
+                                      caps->v1.sampler.bitmask, false)))
+         return false;
    }
 
    if (bind & PIPE_BIND_DEPTH_STENCIL) {
